{"units": [{"id": "daktylodiktos-quote1", "translation": "He, holding in his hands the silkworm's cocoon, the work of the lathe, fills the finger-pointed melody."}, {"id": "daktylodiktos-word1", "translation": "finger-pointed"}, {"id": "dalos-orth1", "translation": "firebrand"}, {"id": "dalos-def1", "translation": "firebrand"}, {"id": "dalos-quote1", "translation": "burning the blood-red firebrand, the age-mate of her child."}, {"id": "damazo-orth1", "translation": "to tame, to subdue"}, {"id": "damazo-def1", "translation": "to tame, to subdue"}, {"id": "damazo-def2", "translation": "Aorist passive ἐδάμην (as if from the unused present form δάμνημι)."}, {"id": "damazo-note1", "translation": "οὐ δαμάζεται [is not subdued] is corrupt for οὐδάμ᾽ ἅζεται [never reverences]. See ἅζομαι [to stand in awe of]."}, {"id": "damazo-note2", "translation": "Another aorist form is δμηθῆναι [to be subdued], whose imperative δμηθήτω [let him be subdued] Homer uses; the Doric participial form δμαθείς [having been subdued] occurs several times in Euripides, once in Aeschylus."}, {"id": "damazo-quote1", "translation": "The spirit of the dead man the fierce jaw of fire does not subdue."}, {"id": "damazo-quote2", "translation": "when the city is subdued."}, {"id": "damazo-quote3", "translation": "lest the city be subdued."}, {"id": "damazo-quote4", "translation": "the army subdued by naval assaults."}, {"id": "damazo-quote5", "translation": "struck by the god's all-scourging whip, he was subdued."}, {"id": "damazo-quote6", "translation": "another subdued in adamant-bound toils."}, {"id": "damazo-quote7", "translation": "subdued by wrathful counsels."}, {"id": "damazo-quote8", "translation": "of those subdued by woman-slaying Ares."}, {"id": "damazo-quote9", "translation": "the most kindly guard having been subdued."}, {"id": "damazo-quote10", "translation": "subdued by a treacherous fate."}, {"id": "damazo-quote11", "translation": "thus to be subdued."}, {"id": "damazo-quote12", "translation": "subdued greatly by sea-borne blows."}, {"id": "damazo-word1", "translation": "is not subdued"}, {"id": "damazo-word2", "translation": "never reverences"}, {"id": "damazo-word3", "translation": "to stand in awe of"}, {"id": "damazo-word4", "translation": "I was subdued"}, {"id": "damazo-word5", "translation": "I subdue"}, {"id": "damazo-word6", "translation": "to be subdued"}, {"id": "damazo-word7", "translation": "let him be subdued"}, {"id": "damazo-word8", "translation": "having been subdued"}, {"id": "damalis-orth1", "translation": "heifer"}, {"id": "damalis-def1", "translation": "heifer"}, {"id": "damalis-quote1", "translation": "like a heifer chased by wolves."}, {"id": "damar-orth1", "translation": "wife"}, {"id": "damar-def1", "translation": "wife"}, {"id": "damar-quote1", "translation": "you led Hesione, persuading her to be your wife, sharing your bed."}, {"id": "damar-quote2", "translation": "or is he roused from his throne by his wife?"}, {"id": "damar-quote3", "translation": "the glorious wife of Zeus."}, {"id": "damar-quote4", "translation": "Hera the fulfiller, the wedded wife of Zeus."}, {"id": "damios-orth1", "translation": "public (Doric for δήμιος)"}, {"id": "damios-def1", "translation": "Doric for δήμιος, which see."}, {"id": "damios-word1", "translation": "public"}, {"id": "damnamai-orth1", "translation": "to subdue (middle form)"}, {"id": "damnamai-def1", "translation": "(middle form) to subdue"}, {"id": "damnamai-def2", "translation": "Passive: to be subdued"}, {"id": "damnamai-quote1", "translation": "the heavenly race is subdued."}, {"id": "damnamai-quote2", "translation": "Alas, leaders and champions of the city, I am subdued."}, {"id": "danaides-orth1", "translation": "Danaids, daughters of Danaus"}, {"id": "danaides-def1", "translation": "Danaids, daughters of Danaus; this was the title of a tragedy by Aeschylus (fr. 40-42b). The chorus of Danaids is in the Suppliants."}, {"id": "danaides-word1", "translation": "of the Danaids"}, {"id": "danaios-orth1", "translation": "long-lasting (Doric for δηναιός)"}, {"id": "danaios-def1", "translation": "Doric for δηναιός, which see."}, {"id": "danaios-word1", "translation": "long-lasting"}, {"id": "danaoï-orth1", "translation": "Greeks"}, {"id": "danaoï-def1", "translation": "Greeks"}, {"id": "danaoï-quote1", "translation": "to the Danaans and Trojans alike."}, {"id": "danaoï-quote2", "translation": "lest you create any adverse winds, remoras against the Danaans."}, {"id": "danaoï-quote3", "translation": "one woman destroying the lives of many Danaan men."}, {"id": "danaoï-quote4", "translation": "the spear-wasted toils of the Danaans."}, {"id": "danaos-orth1", "translation": "Danaus"}, {"id": "danaos-def1", "translation": "Danaus, son of Belus, in the play Suppliants, in which he is named four times."}, {"id": "danaos-quote1", "translation": "Danaus is father and leader of the council."}, {"id": "danaos-quote2", "translation": "Danaus, and he is a brother with fifty sons."}, {"id": "danaos-quote3", "translation": "father Danaus, of good courage."}, {"id": "danaos-quote4", "translation": "Danaus allotted a servant's dowry."}, {"id": "dapedon-orth1", "translation": "ground, floor"}, {"id": "dapedon-def1", "translation": "[corrupt for γάπεδον, which see.]"}, {"id": "dapedon-word1", "translation": "ground, floor"}, {"id": "dapto-orth1", "translation": "to devour, to tear"}, {"id": "dapto-def1", "translation": "to eat, to devour"}, {"id": "dapto-def2", "translation": "to tear"}, {"id": "dapto-note1", "translation": "corrupt for άμαλαπτομέναν [being softened]. See ἀμαλάπτω [to soften]."}, {"id": "dapto-quote1", "translation": "rivers of fire devouring with wild jaws the smooth fields of fair-fruited Sicily."}, {"id": "dapto-quote2", "translation": "I tear my tender cheek warmed by the Nile."}, {"id": "dapto-quote3", "translation": "and in grief I am devoured in my heart."}, {"id": "dapto-quote4", "translation": "being devoured by marriage"}, {"id": "dapto-word1", "translation": "being softened"}, {"id": "dapto-word2", "translation": "to soften"}, {"id": "dareian-orth1", "translation": "Darian (corrupt for Δαριάν)"}, {"id": "dareian-def1", "translation": "[corrupt for Δαριάν, which see.]"}, {"id": "dareian-word1", "translation": "Darian"}, {"id": "dareiogenes-orth1", "translation": "born of Darius"}, {"id": "dareiogenes-def1", "translation": "born of Darius"}, {"id": "dareiogenes-quote1", "translation": "Xerxes the king, born of Darius."}, {"id": "dareios-orth1", "translation": "Darius"}, {"id": "dareios-def1", "translation": "Darius"}, {"id": "dareios-note1", "translation": "and the remaining cases"}, {"id": "dareios-quote1", "translation": "Darius"}, {"id": "darian-orth1", "translation": "Darian (i.e. Darius)"}, {"id": "darian-def1", "translation": "i.e. Δαρεῖος, Darius."}]}